\details{
In a reporting tree, cohorts nest: any two cohorts
either do not overlap, or one contains the other.
Reporting trees arise, for instance, in
tables of year of birth, where most records have detailed cohorts (\code{"1960-1970"}), some have only broad cohorts (\code{"<1960"}), and some have no cohort at all (\code{"Missing"}).

Levels are ordered as follows:
\enumerate{
\item Leaves, that is, cohorts that contain no other cohort,
ordered by lower limit.
\item Broader cohorts, ordered so that each cohort comes
after all cohorts inside it, and before any cohort
entirely above it.
\item Labels that could not be interpreted, if \code{interpret_fail}
is \code{"warn"} or \code{"silent"}.
\item Labels for missing values, as defined by \code{\link[=cohort_is_missing]{cohort_is_missing()}}.
}

Different labels that describe the same cohort are placed
next to each other, in the order in which they first appear.
Leaves do not need to lie at the same depth in the tree.

\code{cohort_set_order_tree()} checks only that the cohorts nest.
It does not check, for instance, that the leaves cover the
full span. Labels for totals, as defined by \code{\link[=cohort_is_total]{cohort_is_total()}}, are
not part of a reporting tree, and cause an error.
Some tables use \code{"Total"} to mean cohort not stated. In such
cases, recode the total to \code{"Missing"} before calling
\code{cohort_set_order_tree()}, for instance using
\code{x[cohort_is_total(x)] <- "Missing"}.

\code{cohort_set_order_tree()} has no effect on the
values of \code{labels}. Only the levels are changed.
